  "prompt_text": "This figure illustrates a proposed gut–brain pathway involving levodopa and dopamine in the stomach and bloodstream. The central comparison is that gastrointestinally derived levodopa, shown as blue dots, can enter the blood and cross the blood–brain barrier to reach the brain, whereas gastrointestinally derived dopamine, shown in red, appears unable to cross that barrier.\n\nIn the stomach lining, epithelial and parietal cells are arranged around gastric glands, with nearby immune cells and enteric neurons. The image suggests that gut microbes, specifically *Helicobacter pylori*, interact closely with the gastric epithelium and may contribute to local production or handling of these molecules. Red and blue particles are shown moving through the stomach tissue, with levodopa passing upward into circulation and then toward the brain. In contrast, dopamine enters the bloodstream but is blocked at the blood–brain barrier, emphasized by a red arrow ending at an X.\n\nOverall, the figure conveys that the gut may generate both levodopa and dopamine, but only levodopa has visible access to the brain. It highlights a stomach-centered interface among microbes, epithelial cells, immune cells, and enteric neurons that could influence neuroactive compounds reaching the central nervous system.",
